```

`STRING_SWITCH_L(p)` reads 4 bytes from `p`. If `p` points near the end of the buffer, this reads past the buffer.

But `p = header_start[i]` which was set from `next_chr` which was found by `memchr` within the buffer. So `p` is within the buffer. But if `p` is within 3 bytes of the end of the buffer, reading 4 bytes from `p` would overflow.

The buffer is `data_copy[32767]` in the fuzzer. The input is copied into `data_copy` with `memcpy(data_copy, data, length)`. Then `buffer_end = data_copy + length`. So the last valid byte is `data_copy[length - 1]`.

If a header line starts at `data_copy[length - 3]` (e.g., "X\r\n"), then `p = data_copy[length - 3]`, and `STRING_SWITCH_L(p)` reads 4 bytes from `data_copy[length - 3]`, which reads `data_copy[length-3]`, `data_copy[length-2]`, `data_copy[length-1]`, and `data_copy[length]` — the last one is out of bounds!

But wait, the `read_request_finalizer` requires `\r\n\r\n` to be present. So the buffer must have at least 4 bytes for the terminator. And the `parse_headers` loop finds `\r` characters. If the last `\r` is at position `length - 2` (the first `\r` of `\r\n\r\n`), then `next_chr` would point to some position before that.

